Below is a practical CBA (Certificate-Based Authentication) test pack you can use for Microsoft Entra ID plus the common relying parties you mentioned: Exchange hybrid, Azure Portal, Teams, and Microsoft 365 (Office.com / O365 apps). I’m assuming Microsoft Entra CBA (not “AD FS only” smartcard) and that you’re using Conditional Access to require/allow certificates.


---

1) Basic Entra ID CBA login scenarios (core)

A. Happy-path interactive sign-in

1. CBA success – correct user cert

Preconditions: Entra CBA enabled; cert chain trusted; user cert mapped (UPN/SAN or altSecurityIdentities); CA policy allows CBA.

Steps: Browse to https://myaccount.microsoft.com → sign in → select certificate.

Expected: Sign-in succeeds; Entra sign-in logs show Authentication Method = Certificate; CA applied as expected.



2. CBA success – multiple certs presented

Steps: User has 2 valid certs (correct + other) → sign in → select correct cert.

Expected: Succeeds only with correct cert; wrong selection fails with clear error.



3. CBA success – cert with UPN in SAN

Expected: Correct mapping; no prompt to “pick account” (depends on config).



4. CBA success – cert mapped via altSecurityIdentities

Expected: Sign-in succeeds and logs show cert auth.




B. Negative / security scenarios

5. Invalid chain – untrusted issuer

Steps: Present cert issued by non-trusted CA.

Expected: Authentication fails; logs indicate untrusted chain.



6. Expired certificate

Expected: Fails; clear “expired” reason in logs.



7. Revoked certificate (CRL/OCSP reachable)

Expected: Fails; revocation reason appears in logs.



8. Revocation check unreachable

Expected: Behavior matches your configured policy (fail-open vs fail-closed). Document expected result explicitly.



9. Certificate doesn’t map to a user

Steps: Valid cert but no mapping to Entra identity.

Expected: Fails; “no matching user” type result.



10. Wrong user cert (maps to different user)



Expected: Entra signs in as mapped identity; verify this is blocked if your policy requires the intended user context (common governance test).


11. Key usage mismatch



Steps: Present cert without Client Authentication EKU (or wrong template).

Expected: Fails.


12. Private key not accessible



Steps: Cert present but key missing/not accessible (bad install).

Expected: Fails with client-side error; no successful sign-in.


C. Conditional Access + MFA interaction scenarios

13. CBA satisfies MFA (if configured)



Steps: CA policy requires MFA; confirm whether CBA counts as MFA in your tenant settings/policy design.

Expected: Either (a) CBA alone satisfies, or (b) user still prompted for additional factor — verify intended outcome.


14. CBA + compliant device required



Preconditions: Intune device compliance + CA.

Expected: Compliant device passes; non-compliant blocked (even with valid cert).


15. CBA blocked from unmanaged device



Steps: Attempt from unmanaged BYOD.

Expected: Block or limited session per CA.


16. Location-based policy



Steps: Allowed location succeeds; disallowed location blocked (still with valid cert).


D. Risk / Identity Protection (if used)

17. High sign-in risk blocks CBA



Expected: Block or require remediation even though certificate is valid.


18. User risk policy triggers password reset



Expected: Verify flow (note: CBA users may have specific remediation UX constraints).



